It’s almost 4 a.m. on Saturday, and Whale-chan still isn’t asleep. Let’s switch to a slightly more niche topic today: privacy coin $ZEC.

First, a look at the market (Binance spot data): $BTC  about $59,890, down about 1.4% over 24 hours; #ETH  about $1,671, down about 2.1%; ZEC about $743, up about 3.7% over 24 hours—one of the few in the green tonight. The Fear & Greed Index is at 12: extreme fear.

This month has actually been pretty rough for ZEC. Here are a few things I found:
1) According to several reports, ZEC fell more than 40% cumulatively in November, at one point returning to around $700. Traders have generally been watching the $670–700 area for support;
2) On November 26, the Zcash development team recommended disabling new deposits into Sapling, the privacy pool launched in 2018, and migrating funds to the newer Ironwood pool. The reason is that the cryptography used by Sapling is theoretically vulnerable to quantum computing. There are currently no known vulnerabilities, and withdrawals from Sapling remain open. Reports say there are still about 376,000–400,000 ZEC in Sapling, while Ironwood already holds around 85% of shielded funds;
3) That same week, Gemini announced support for shielded deposits into the Ironwood pool. Together with shielded withdrawals, which it had already enabled, this makes it the first major exchange to support both (on-chain privacy; identity verification on the exchange remains unchanged).

My take (hmph, it’s definitely not because I want to hide little fish treats in a privacy pool):
· “Proactive migration” is a defensive security upgrade. It may cause some short-term market jitters, but it doesn’t mean the coin has been compromised—don’t let the headlines scare you;
· Seen against the backdrop of a 40% monthly decline, tonight’s rebound looks more like a breather after being oversold. A green day alone doesn’t tell us anything about the trend;
· Privacy-focused assets are more volatile and face greater regulatory uncertainty, so keep your positions more restrained than with major coins.

When the tide goes out, even little fish shouldn’t go nosing around in random reef crevices~

For informational purposes only; this is not investment advice.

